{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/adr/0001-native-operation-authority.md docs/adr/0002-academy-shared-presentation.md docs/adr/0003-native-harness-and-scoped-identity.md docs/adr/0004-managed-workbench-admission.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/adr/0001-native-operation-authority.md docs/adr/0002-academy-shared-presentation.md docs/adr/0003-native-harness-and-scoped-identity.md docs/adr/0004-managed-workbench-admission.md'","aggregated_output":"# 1. Native operation authority owns coordination\n\nDate: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict\nand implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D008,D009,D013,D014,D016,D019.\n\nV1 already has deep domain/request modules, but GPUI callers still coordinate\nslots, protected-key IO and ordered effects. React must not inherit that authority.\n\nChoose a principal-bound Rust actor with dispatch(Intent) and observe(ViewScope).\nKeep feature transitions domain-owned inside one initial core crate. Native\nbootstrap injects adapters and creates operator/Harness handles; JSON cannot\nselect the caller principal. Finite domain instructions exclude arbitrary SDK\nmethod strings and any Harness Apply capability.\n\nThe interface includes atomic admission/context retirement, per-source supersession,\nseparate read/review/write/stream domains, priority/deadline/rate-limit semantics,\nbounded queues, bounded delivery-sequence deduplication with safe session rotation,\nsingle-use settlement and truthful Remote outcome. No remote IO\nor blocking vault call occurs while the authoritative transition is locked.\nAdmitted work runs on the host's one async runtime; native adapters execute effects.\n\nReact owns raw editor text/undo. Rust owns immutable captured drafts/reviews.\nReview/Apply/Save/Send capture fresh input in their actual production handlers;\nApply compares fresh input with the stored reviewed operation and executes only\nthat stored operation. Invalid/lossy input, changed context/draft/resource or a\nconsumed review refuses dispatch. Never rely only on a debounced revision.\nRequestApply uses a native operator confirmation; actual Apply completion is\nprivate, single-use and revalidated after the modal editor/context lease.\nEvery scoped intent/observation carries native-issued context and principal\nadmission. Async vault completions are generation/lease-bound; obsolete writes\nreceive orphan cleanup rather than reactivating credentials. Explicit one-time\nsecret reveal/copy stays in a scoped transient native dialog, never snapshots.\n\nAlternatives considered under the design-it-twice skill:\n\n| Design | Depth/locality | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Actor dispatch/observe | Callers learn one authority seam; context and settlement stay together | Finite unions and observation backpressure need discipline |\n| Typed scoped handles | Strong domain methods and revoked scopes; flexible internal composition | More handles/methods and IPC registries for callers to coordinate |\n| Task-oriented scan board | Trivial first scan workflow | Public task surface grows across all parity features |\n\nRecommend the actor with domain-specific types and a small generated presentation\nfacade. Retain the alternatives' explicit scoped validation and effortless scan\nusage internally. Do not combine all their public surfaces or add pass-through\nmodules merely to obtain two method names.\n\nTwo real execution adapters (SDK/scripted), storage adapters (Keychain/session/\nfault-injectable) and clocks justify internal seams. The interface is the test\nsurface: switch/replace/cancel while queued/dispatched, old/duplicate reply,\nfresh-input races, double Apply, denied Harness Apply, unknown outcome/no replay,\npriority/deadline/rate-limit containment and observation resynchronization.\n\nSee [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md) for ownership/wire details.\nNo app code has been written and no native/SDK test is claimed by this ADR.\n# 2. Share Academy presentation through explicit composition\n\nDate: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict\nand implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D004,D005,D006,D007,D010.\n\nThe current UI package re-exports web app source. Shell palette/mobile navigation\nand theme persistence require tRPC/Clerk/router context; the authenticated web\nentry also starts learner and manager behavior. A whole-app import would spread\nthat authority into unrelated desktop features.\n\nKeep one Academy-owned presentation implementation. Create explicit provider-free\ncontrols/theme/shell exports while preserving the existing tooling entry. Both\nAcademy web and desktop consume the extracted source. Composition adapters supply\nbrand/navigation/account/header/palette/mobile slots; the shared module owns\nvisual semantics, responsive layout and focus. No universal plugin framework.\n\nDesktop global theme is an app preference stored through Rust, not hosted\nsettings.update. Academy web keeps its learner-account theme behavior. Learner\nnotifications, gamification and account effects mount only in Academy composition.\n\nExtract learner views separately when C05/C06 require them. Retain React Query/\ntRPC hooks behind a transport/navigation provider; type-only learner contracts\nbuild without the Express/DB runtime. Web uses existing HTTP authentication;\ndesktop uses a terminating link into the allowlisted Rust Academy transport.\nTokens stay native. Hosted learning/access/grading/award truth and client-safe\nbundle/draft contracts remain authoritative. Never bundle raw answer content.\n\nDirectly embedding the authenticated web root is rejected because its interface\ncontains unrelated account/manager assumptions. Maintaining a desktop UI copy\nis rejected because appearance and learner behavior would diverge.\n\nTests render shared presentation without hosted identity/query providers, run\nboth consumers' regressions, verify native keyboard/focus/themes, and prove no\nlearner mutation/gamification effect from general desktop screens. Learner tests\ncover native link envelopes/allowlist, account cache retirement and hosted authority.\n\nDependency/source/license pins are extraction prerequisites. This ADR changes\nno Academy checkout, package or hosted registration. See\n[selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md).\n# 3. Retain native Harness and own scoped identity in Rust\n\nDate: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict\nand implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D010,D011,D014–D016,D021–D023.\n\nDesktop-server is already a Rust engine. V1 host workers and its package manifest\nstill require Node/managed CLI. Preserve engine depth and replace those host\nrequirements, rather than rewrite the agent. Source inspection alone does not\nestablish that a newly sealed installed bundle works without the old resources.\n\nNative lifecycle owns sealed executable selection, bounded stdin bootstrap,\nprotocol supervision, process cleanup, scoped grants and protected history.\nReact only presents lifecycle state. Grant/tenant/project changes retire the\nrun; approvals bind actual call items. Preserve existing project/process/tool\nrestrictions, Gateway-only inference/MCP and no engine Apply.\n\nIdentity shares native discovery/HTTP/validation/storage mechanics while retaining\nAcademy learner, Harness inference and MCP grant policies. They are distinct\nidentities with distinct sign-out/refresh/context effects. Use public-client\nPKCE/device flows and a no-redirect native transport; never embed a client secret\nor trust renderer-selected issuer/token destinations.\n\nHost signature/JWKS validation is a deliberate stricter v2 requirement; v1\nomitted it. Validate ID/access token types according to their actual contracts,\nnot by decoding arbitrary JWT claims. Rotation/expiry/sleep/revocation fail closed.\nSame-identity token refresh restarts the one-shot-bootstrap engine and restores\ncompatible in-session history; it re-confirms workspace-write permission and\nnever replays remote/tool effects. Declining workspace-write confirmation keeps\nthe draft/cancels Send without restart. Any changed ContextBinding (including\nlocal access/model/configuration/MCP inventory) starts a new conversation; its\nold history is inspect-only under the new binding. MCP-only token rotation also\nrebootstraps/restores an identical binding, but retains working v1's current\nproject-access confirmation without an SSO refresh or another write prompt.\nNative scope additionally binds per-server MCP grant IDs/catalog revisions absent\nfrom engine equality; re-sign-in/sign-out/removal/revision change retires the\nconversation. Expiry alone leaves inference usable with that non-required server\nunavailable and native calls denied. This continuity is distinct from inspect-only\nimported history. No token or refresh credential enters React, diagnostics, process arguments or\ninherited environment. One-time operator-entered inputs remain transient.\n\nUse independent v2 bundle/data/Keychain namespaces. Protected work is encrypted\nand account/context-scoped; no plaintext fallback. Reviewed import creates a new\nv2 data generation, never modifies v1, transfers no credentials/approvals and\nretains a restartable receipt/rollback generation. Historical conversations are\ninspect-only until fresh-context compatibility is qualified.\n\nTests cross native process and identity interfaces: malformed/oversized bootstrap,\nqueue pressure, wrong context, crash/EOF/graceful and forced cleanup, signature/\nissuer/audience/key-rotation rejection, scoped sign-out, protected histories and\nNode-free installed inventory. Native/live/final signed-byte proof remain separate.\nSee [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md) for identity/crypto proposals.\n# 4. Managed HTTP enters native configuration authority\n\nDate: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict\nand implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D014,D016 and C14/C15 requirements.\n\nV1's workbench has real request-document and transport depth. Its redactions-based\nredirect guard is not explicit tenant-credential classification, Live mode lacks\napplication destination binding, and its post-401 retry can resend a mutation.\nThe stricter v2 review/no-replay policy must be enforced below every Send path.\n\nKeep RequestDocument reconciliation separate. Native execution admission captures\na fresh immutable effective request and explicitly distinguishes tenant-managed,\nworkbench-owned and anonymous credentials. Known managed reads need capability/\ndestination admission. Known mutations need exact Configuration review. Unknown\nmanaged methods/paths are denied, even if presented as a read or generic draft.\nOrdinary workbench-owned/anonymous HTTP remains supported.\n\nReplace the editable SCM collection-ID credential gate with native-owned\ncapability registration. Imported IDs/references cannot authorize tenant secret\nresolution. An edited token_url/request URL is admitted against the explicit\ncredential destination before secrets are obtained; an ID change never creates\na capability. Preserve operator-reviewed endpoint configuration separately.\n\nBind review to expanded URL/method/headers/body and file hashes, variables,\ncookies, credential/context/destination and resource classification. Recheck\nbefore dispatch; changed input/context/files require fresh review. Rust constructs\nthe actual request; renderer fields cannot mark it preapproved.\n\nManaged redirects are disabled initially: a 3xx is shown, and following it is a\nnew prepared/admitted request. Generic redirects preserve bounded cookie/credential\norigin policy. No automatic workbench resend follows HTTP401, refresh, reconnect,\nlost response, import or history replay. Refresh can precede a new explicit Send.\n\nThe built-in catalogue has 193 request specifications, 81 with mutation methods;\nthis is metadata parity, not a claim of dedicated/live-qualified product support.\nTests cover imported/edited requests, unknown managed endpoints, direct IPC bypass,\nambiguous paths/auth headers, same/cross-origin redirects, stale cookie/file/\nvariable/context bindings, double Apply and post-401/lost-response no resend.\n\nThis is a stricter v2 product contract, not an allegation that v1 failed its own\ncontract. See [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/planning/WORF-START-HERE.md docs/planning/status.md'","aggregated_output":"# Continue the current desktop work in the Worf VM\n\nThis is the 2026-11-01 continuation guide. It supersedes the old sibling\n`p09-queue/handoff` instructions about Geordi, rsync and the older C44 worktree.\nThe target is the existing macOS VM on **Worf**. Kirk's local Tart VM `airs`\nwas only a bootstrap experiment; it produced no passing P09 native acceptance.\nIts Python permission prompt is not a prerequisite for this Git migration.\n\n## Get the current source\n\nFrom an empty development directory in the Worf VM:\n\n```sh\ngit clone --branch main ssh://git@git-ssh.cdot.io/cdot/prisma-airs-desktop-v2.git\ncd prisma-airs-desktop-v2\ngit lfs install --local\ngit lfs pull\npython3 -I tools/p09/verify-migration.py\ngit rev-parse HEAD origin/main\n```\n\nFor an existing clean clone, use `git fetch origin`, `git switch main`,\n`git pull --ff-only origin main`, then `git lfs pull` and the same verification.\nPreserve local modifications before changing an existing checkout.\nThe verification checks the latest application/tooling source, all 221 previously\npending main files, every retained worktree file mapping, required commit ancestry\nand the historical L19 executable. It intentionally describes this migration\ncheckpoint; later intentional implementation changes will change its hashes.\n\nAll chunk branch tips are retained as remote branches. C37/C39/C39b/C40/C41/\nC42/C43/C44 were already merged. The C29 live and C44 scope commits were applied\nas equivalent cherry-picks. The older `p09-merge` history and detached dry merge\nare preserved as refs rather than replacing the newer integrated source.\nThe audit and exact file mapping are in\n[worktree-preservation/2026-11-01](worktree-preservation/2026-11-01/README.md).\nNo original worktree was deleted or reset.\n\n## Prepare build inputs\n\nUse macOS Apple Silicon with a logged-in GUI account and Xcode Command Line\nTools/Swift. The contracted toolchain is Rust **1.99.0** (rustfmt, clippy,\n`aarch64-apple-darwin`), Node **24.21.0** in its NVM directory, pnpm **10.6.5**,\nPython 3, and Git LFS. Install those in the VM before running the following:\n\n```sh\nmkdir -p .tools/bin\ncorepack enable pnpm --install-directory .tools/bin\nexport PATH=\"$PWD/.tools/bin:$HOME/.cargo/bin:$HOME/.nvm/versions/node/v24.21.0/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin\"\ncargo fetch --locked\ngit clone --branch v2/desktop-presentation ssh://git@git-ssh.cdot.io/cdot.io/ai-security-academy.git ../ai-security-academy\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- fetch-inputs --source ../ai-security-academy\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- shared-prepare --input .inputs/academy --scratch .work/academy\npnpm install --frozen-lockfile --ignore-scripts\npnpm --dir .work/academy install --frozen-lockfile --ignore-scripts\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- shared-lifecycle --allowlist docs/architecture/shared-lifecycle.json --scratch .work/academy\npnpm --dir .work/academy exec playwright install chromium --only-shell\n```\n\n`fetch-inputs` uses the locked Academy commit **532809aa4bb2e1484e11ca9f7a414328260c7901**,\nnot the evolving branch tip. Keep the input lock unchanged for the move.\nThe Rust SCM SDK is the locked crates.io dependency; the Harness is the pinned\nrepository LFS archive. Neither needs an external source worktree. Recreate\nbuild/dependency directories here; do not restore Kirk's absolute symlinks.\n\nUse **Terminal.app** as the guest GUI execution context. Resolve its actual\nAccessibility/System Events permission prompts in the VM. A 1920×1200 display\nis suitable for the native matrix. Configure the Codex CLI/model in the VM for\nfresh read-only reviews; host authentication files are not part of this transfer.\nThe owner's planning skill is preserved at `tools/p09/skills/grill-me/SKILL.md`\nand can be installed under the guest's `~/.codex/skills/grill-me/`.\n\n## Exact current standing and next work\n\n- **C44 attempt-05: 9/9/9/9/9**, no required fixes/blockers/gaps; merged. Its\n  opaque collection scope, render-time stale-editor retirement, packet isolation\n  and complete legacy-map guard are in current source.\n- **L19: actual Vulture wizard verification passed** on attempt-03 after the\n  separately approved local credential correction. The original two reads and\n  separately approved third are charged, with **no remaining live allowance**.\n  Do not repeat the live probe. Redacted receipts and the exact historical\n  executable (LFS) are retained; raw build paths/receipts/verdicts are unchanged.\n- **C29 attempt-07: 9/9/9/8/8**, no blockers, accepted under D034 with R1/R2/E1/E2.\n  Commit `03e572be` restores Gateway/key setup, rejected/fixed/stale verification,\n  keyed Cancel/Keychain cleanup, relaunch/import/confinement checks and the full\n  **24-behavior, 48-row** wizard matrix. Syntax and completeness-map checks pass.\n  The new native flow has **not** passed yet.\n- **P09 check attempt-02: all 18 checks passed** on unchanged inputs (541 frontend\n  tests, 15 live-scope tests, 57 synthetic live-run tests, four L19 driver tests).\n  Those records are Kirk history. The current app/build inputs match those tested\n  bytes, including the owner work now committed for this move.\n- C30/C31/C32 still need their predecessor-dependent re-reviews after C29 reaches\n  full 9. The P09 phase review follows full native/check/member completion.\n\nArchive the retained Kirk P09 preparation/incomplete native generation in a new\nhistory directory before creating the Worf generation. Keep all verdict folders\nimmutable and choose fresh check/review attempt IDs. Then run:\n\n```sh\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- native-prepare --phase P09 --target aarch64-apple-darwin --features test-fixtures\npython3 -I docs/planning/evidence/P09/native-controller/run-p09-native.py c36\npython3 -I docs/planning/evidence/P09/native-controller/run-p09-native.py c43\n```\n\nC36 proves the guest driver context; C43 proves the restored C29 native coverage.\nContinue the other required drivers serially using the retained native controller\nplan and assertion requirements. Every record must bind the newly prepared\nexecutable. C44's cumulative driver runs last. Historical C24-N06 and C31-N01/N04\naggregates remain superseded as the map states; their old raw failures are not\nrewritten or counted as passing checks.\n\nUse a fresh P09 check attempt (next currently `attempt-03`) and the portable\nhelpers in [tools/p09](../../tools/p09/README.md) for C29 attempt-08, then C30,\nC31 and C32 in order. Report every actual score and apply D034's 8-then-9 rule.\nThe copied phase packet builder is explicitly a disabled draft; finish its\ncurrent-P09 native/predecessor verification before using it for the phase review.\nC26 still requires the documented manual review fallback; do not manufacture\ncontroller completion.\n\nAfter the P09 phase passes at 9, execute the owner-approved\n[C45/P10 brief](C45-draft.md): Prisma AIRS Desktop, version 1.0.0,\n`io.cdot.airs-desktop` / `.fixture`, fresh setup with no alpha/v2 migration,\nlegacy/variant/import cleanup, chunk 8→9 and phase 9. C45 has not started.\nThe preserved pending 2.0.0 version/release work is superseded at that later\nchunk, not silently renamed during migration. October C26 installed/signing\nrecords and `docs/release/2.0.0.md` are historical/draft; pushing source neither\npublishes a release nor clears their remaining gates.\n\nPrivate live manifests, Keychains, CLI tenant TOMLs, signing keys and authentication\nprofiles stay outside Git. They are not needed for the next fixture gates.\n# Current state\n\nG00 and G01 passed (historical Claude reviews). C01–C04 and the P01 cumulative review completed on\n2026-10-17; C05–C07 and the P02 cumulative review completed on 2026-10-18;\nC08–C12 and the P03 cumulative review completed on 2026-10-19; C13–C15 and\nthe P04 cumulative review completed on 2026-10-20; C16–C18 and the P05\ncumulative review completed on 2026-10-21; C19–C22 and the P06 cumulative\nreview completed on 2026-10-23; C23–C24 and the P07 cumulative review\ncompleted on 2026-10-24. P08/C26 remains open; the owner-authorized P09\nintegration is now at fresh Worf cumulative native qualification after C44 full-9 closure.\nWorf native generation 01 passed all 19 mapped drivers with C44 last, including\nC43's 24 behaviors and 48 rows. It is preserved complete with the exact executable\nunder `evidence/P09/native-history/Worf-generation-01-complete-20261101`.\nFresh check attempt-03 passed 17/18 checks but failed import test relaunch/timing\nassertions on all three fixture runs. The corrected harness uses the production\nshutdown hook, a durable receipt boundary and a read-only fixture observer of the\narmed cut; the full import suite now passes 23/23 with every assertion retained.\nNative generation 02 is rebuilt with an exact before/after input seal; C36 passed,\nand all remaining mapped drivers will repeat before\nfresh checks and C29–C32/P09 reviews. C24-N06 and C31-N01/N04 remain superseded raw\nfailures under the unchanged map. No current native or review pass is transferred\nto changed inputs.\nUse [WORF-START-HERE](WORF-START-HERE.md) for the 2026-11-01 checkpoint and next commands. D026 (2026-10-17) makes Codex the independent reviewer of every chunk\nand of a cumulative review after every phase, each requiring at least 9 in all\nfive dimensions with empty required fixes, blockers and evidence gaps. D034 (2026-10-18, owner rule) lets a chunk complete at 8/10 with no\nblockers; each phase review still needs 9/10, after every chunk accepted below\n9 is raised to a passing review.\n\n| Gate/chunk | State | Evidence/next action |\n|---|---|---|\n| G00 | Passed (historical) | Attempt03 scored 9 in all five dimensions |\n| G01 | Passed (historical) | Attempt05 scored 9 in all five