26 remains in progress; every chunk is reviewed until it scores 9 in every dimension.\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2407:   phase P09 chunks C27–C34 (see the chunk index). The first implementation\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2413:3. D014 holds: secrets enter only through the host secret sheet and vault;\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2433:6. The wizard realises D013 (scan key first and only required credential);\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2438:   at 100%/200% in both themes per chunk. Live calls only in C29 (verify,\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2446:   redacted wire data; English only in P09; chunks commit on main except C34.\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2451:## D059 — P09 re-cut: port the prototype, one gate per screen at 8\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2458:- The frozen prototype (D058, version 134) is the specification per screen; the\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2474:  over the existing state machine, C44 legacy removal on a branch and the P09\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2477:## Directions explicitly requested before round 1\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2490:## Interview exit and remaining evidence\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2502:## D060 — No import from the alpha; this desktop is v1\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2507:- Still to decide the same way, in a follow-up chunk the owner names (proposed C45): the core's alpha-import intents and reader (`import_v1_domain`, the `V1Import*` intents, the host adapters), Settings' file-based \"Import tenant profiles\" and the API Client's \"Import v1 API Client profile\" adoption, which are alpha-import surfaces too; their removal changes contracts and earlier chunks' tests and is not done inside the P09 closure.\ndocs/planning/decisions.md:2508:- Naming: this desktop becomes v1 and the previous one the alpha. The rename touches the application identity that lets the two coexist (bundle identifiers, data roots, Keychain namespaces, D001-era identities) and the whole document set; it is its own chunk after P09, not a find-and-replace.\ndocs/planning/test-catalogue.md:1:# Behavioral and native test catalogue\ndocs/planning/test-catalogue.md:9:## Core authority and SDK\ndocs/planning/test-catalogue.md:39:## Presentation and Academy\ndocs/planning/test-catalogue.md:61:## Workflows and Harness\ndocs/planning/test-catalogue.md:86:## Import, quality, and artifacts\ndocs/planning/test-catalogue.md:120:## Performance budget governance\ndocs/planning/parity-matrix.md:1:# Working-v1 parity and release closure\ndocs/planning/parity-matrix.md:36:## Required per-workflow ledger\ndocs/planning/parity-matrix.md:59:## Intentional non-goals and retained classifications\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:1:# Prisma AIRS Desktop v2 implementation plan\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:4:terms and all four architecture candidates; D026 makes Codex the independent\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:8:## Outcome and exclusions\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:25:## Architectural guiding principles\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:59:## Ordered phases and chunks\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:80:| P09   | C27–C44  | UI v2 \"Refined Native\": spec, shell, wizard, destinations (D058); re-cut from C35 as one screen per chunk ported from the prototype (D059) |\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:82:### G00 — Plan confirmation\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:91:### G01 — Architecture improvement workflow\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:104:## Per-chunk execution contract\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:120:   review through [the independent prompt](prompts/review-chunk.md) (D026).\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:131:## Command contract to establish in C01\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:153:## Coverage and evidence\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:172:## Expected sequencing dependencies\ndocs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md:190:## Completion and delivery\nDESIGN.md\nbudgets.json\nchecks.json\ninputs.lock.json\nlive-manifest.template.json\nnative-acceptance.md\nnative-checks.json\nnotice-inventory.json\nqualification.md\nreview-verdict.schema.json\nshared-lifecycle.json\ntoolchain.json\nui-v2\nworkflow-ledger.json\nworkflows.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"The request maps C29’s native requirements to the current C43 wizard driver. I’m checking that mapping against the original acceptance requirements, especially the credential cleanup, verification recovery, and full step matrix missing in attempt-07."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/planning/test-catalogue.md docs/planning/parity-matrix.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/screens/boards.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/planning/test-catalogue.md docs/planning/parity-matrix.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/screens/boards.md'","aggregated_output":"# Behavioral and native test catalogue\n\nThese are required future tests and observable assertions, not results already\nexecuted. G01/C01 bind them to exact commands. Each chunk names its applicable\nIDs and adds feature-specific cases. Use fake transports/clocks/stores at real\nmodule interfaces plus literal external fixtures; native and live tests provide\nseparate evidence. Do not assert implementation-private structure for its own sake.\n\n## Core authority and SDK\n\n- **T01 Context isolation:** tenant/workspace/credential switch during a request\n  rejects old replies, retires pending reviews/one-time secrets, and cancels work.\n  Switching back cannot revive authority. Credential replacement and sign-out\n  behave the same way; duplicate completions settle at most once.\n- **T02 Review authority:** forged/stale/replayed/consumed review IDs, changed\n  draft/resource/context, model text, and imported history cannot Apply. Valid\n  operator Apply transmits the exact reviewed operation once.\n- **T03 Unknown outcomes:** disconnect before send, after send, timeout, cancel,\n  401/403/429/5xx, and partial multistep provisioning yield correct states. No\n  automatic mutation/scan replay; offer readback/review/explicit retry where valid.\n- **T04 SDK external contract:** independently authored request/response fixtures\n  verify method/path/body/headers/options, pagination/multipart limits, unknown\n  enum/action preservation, sanitized errors, and no dropped options.\n- **T05 Credential confinement:** scan-only setup works; management/inference/MCP\n  credentials are separate. Deny wrong-origin redirects or custom endpoint\n  changes that would forward a credential without an explicitly reviewed new\n  destination binding. Preserve legitimate operator-reviewed endpoint overrides\n  and explicitly selected nonsecret tenant-profile import; whitelist metadata,\n  discard embedded secrets and never follow credential-file references.\n  No implicit environment/tenant file\n  loading, upstream fallback, persistent renderer storage, or secret log fields.\n- **T06 Protected storage:** locked/denied/unavailable/corrupt store, interrupted\n  write/reset, session-only mode, restart, explicit one-time reveal/clipboard,\n  and cleanup. No plaintext fallback; v1 namespaces remain untouched.\n- **T07 Lifecycle:** bounded concurrent work, cancellation, queue backpressure,\n  superseding reads, app close/restart, sleep/wake, expired credentials, and\n  monotonic versus wall-clock decisions. UI remains responsive during work.\n\n## Presentation and Academy\n\n- **T08 Shared presentation:** web and desktop consumers build from the same\n  reviewed shared source; themes/fonts/routes render correctly. General desktop\n  screens mount no Academy gamification/account effects or web manager routes.\n- **T09 Native UI:** actual Tauri command path, menus/shortcuts, keyboard-only\n  workflow, focus restoration, VoiceOver labels/order, zoom/large text, themes,\n  reduced motion, long/empty/error content, and window resizing. Mock IPC tests\n  alone cannot establish these. macOS WebDriver availability must be verified;\n  use supported native accessibility/automation or recorded manual steps.\n- **T10 Academy identity:** device grant, pending/slow_down/expiry/cancel, refresh,\n  revocation, wrong issuer/client/scope, account switch/sign-out, and stale replies.\n  Token management stays Rust-owned. The learner account is independent of tenant.\n- **T11 Learning authority:** client bundles contain no answer keys/private lab\n  evaluation, privileged routes, or assessor tokens. Forged verdict/award/local\n  exam clock cannot pass; the hosted backend decides access, grading, and awards.\n  Signed-in denial differs from guest sign-in; sandbox never uses tenant secrets.\n- **T12 Offline work:** download lease/revision/account access, encrypted draft\n  save/restart, locked vault, sign-out/account switch, withdrawn content, duplicate\n  submission IDs, changed draft versions, and reconnection. No deferred action\n  replay. Valid local work remains usable without claiming offline grading.\n\n## Workflows and Harness\n\n- **T13 Collection semantics:** exact capability subsets, large collections,\n  bounded pagination/truncation, search/filter/page resets, stale/partial cache,\n  consistent Home/Inbox source facts, and no false complete/zero/absent claims.\n- **T14 API Client documents:** edit/save/conflict while typing, invalid input,\n  environment/secret separation, import/path traversal/size errors, multipart,\n  cookies/redirect auth, cancellation, history/download, Git/profile adoption,\n  and recovery from interrupted writes. Arbitrary workbench targets cannot borrow\n  privileged application credentials through renderer-supplied references.\n- **T15 Harness process:** actual native binary and bounded bootstrap/JSONL\n  protocol, malformed/oversized frames, correlation/duplicates, queue pressure,\n  crash/EOF/cancel, context change, graceful/forced cleanup, and missing/tampered\n  resources. No PATH launcher fallback or plaintext rollout/history.\n- **T16 Harness authority:** typed investigation/proposal tools, active-draft\n  protection, no engine Apply, one-time secrets absent from model context,\n  read-only project defaults, approved single-process command restrictions,\n  forbidden descendants/pipelines, stale decisions, and history replay.\n- **T17 Inference/MCP identity:** pinned Gateway origin; SSO PKCE/state/nonce/\n  issuer/client/audience/time/signature/JWKS/algorithm/key-rotation validation;\n  sleep/refresh/revoke/identity changes;\n  distinct inference/MCP grants; fixed conversation server scope; exact call-item\n  approvals; changed revision/grant; denied tools; trace/execution evidence.\n  No direct upstream provider fallback or global cross-workspace MCP authority.\n\n## Import, quality, and artifacts\n\n- **T18 Import transaction:** dry-run selection, versions/source lock, account\n  match, protected source reads, malformed/oversized/traversal inputs, interruption\n  at each commit point, restart, duplicate import, destination conflict, rollback,\n  and source-byte preservation. No credentials/sessions/approvals transfer.\n- **T19 Diagnostics privacy:** seeded canary secrets and customer content absent\n  from logs/crash files/exports by default; explicit redacted export; correct\n  retention/reset; no analytics/crash upload attempts; encrypted user history\n  remains a distinct intentional storage feature.\n- **T20 Performance:** named macOS hardware/OS/workloads, cold/warm startup, idle\n  and scan/large-list/streaming/Harness memory, main-thread responsiveness, file\n  and DMG footprint. Record methodology, repetitions, distribution and baseline;\n  set core budgets in C03 and register later workload budgets at the introducing\n  chunk's entry before implementation/acceptance measurement. See the budget\n  governance below; C24/C26 compare with pre-registered values.\n- **T21 Controlled live:** designated account/tenant/entitlements and disposable\n  resources, explicit operation allowlist/budgets, sanitized request IDs, readback,\n  cleanup receipts, deny/error cases, and unresolved outcome handling. Missing\n  access or unsupported operations remain blocked; no destructive production tests.\n- **T22 Installed package:** signed/notarized/stapled DMG, Gatekeeper/quarantine,\n  mount/drag/install/launch/relaunch with fresh user/home and no developer checkout,\n  PATH Node/Bun/managed CLI disabled, actual Rust sidecar, runtime inventory/hash\n  integrity, no hidden backend download, safe uninstall/reset/coexistence,\n  manual update, previous-version rollback, and preserved user work.\n- **T23 Cross-feature offline:** launch offline and read/edit each applicable\n  local category: saved cases, FP/FN reports, API Client documents, protected\n  Harness history, configuration drafts, and Academy work. Mark remote data as\n  stale/unknown as appropriate. Offline drafts do not retain valid Apply authority;\n  reconnect requires fresh readback/review when needed. Pending reviews, scans,\n  inference turns, generic HTTP mutations and MCP/tool calls never resubmit on\n  reconnect. Offer explicit retry while preserving uncertain remote outcomes.\n  Bind to C03,C09–C12,C14–C15,C17–C18,C20–C22 and cumulative C24.\n\n## Performance budget governance\n\nC03 registers numeric budgets and named methods for the shell/scan workloads\nbefore C04. Also register the later workload catalogue and owning chunks.\nBefore implementing/measuring a newly introduced workload, its owning chunk\ncommits a numeric budget, hardware/OS, dataset, measurement method and rationale:\nC11/C13 large collections; C15 large HTTP bodies/downloads; C18 streaming/charts;\nC19 native-engine startup/memory; C20 history; C21/C22 tool/MCP workloads.\nDo not wait until C24 to fit limits to observed results.\n\nA budget change requires a versioned rationale, original failures/results, and\nowner or independent reviewer acknowledgement recorded in the receipt. It cannot\nsilently erase a regression. Benchmark evidence records the pre-registered budget\nhash; the controller rejects retroactive or mismatched budget inputs. C24/C26\ncompare against those exact pre-committed values, including changed-budget history.\n\nNormal bounded metadata reads or credential refresh prerequisites for an explicit\noperation are not deferred business-action replay. Tests must distinguish these\nfrom silently resubmitting scans, writes, grading, or tool calls after reconnect.\n\nBefore review, challenge security-critical tests with a relevant deliberate\nbehavior mutation or equivalent negative probe. Show that violating an invariant\nfails the test; do not create an exhaustive mutation framework or mirror every\nimplementation detail. Run mutations only in isolated disposable copies.\n# Working-v1 parity and release closure\n\nAll v2 rows are **planned**. No v2 source/native/live/artifact result exists yet.\nThe baseline is [feature-baseline.md](feature-baseline.md); do not expand a\npreview/stub into required live behavior without an explicit scope decision.\n\n| Requirement family                     | Owning chunks   | Closure requirement                                                                  |\n|----------------------------------------|-----------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Tenant setup/scan-only/protected state | C02,C03,C08,C23 | Context/capabilities, reviewed endpoint overrides/profile import, verification/reset |\n| Runtime synchronous scan/cases         | C03,C12         | Faithful verdicts, profiles, cancellation and no silent replay                       |\n| Profiles/topics/applications/keys      | C08,C09         | Exact supported reads/writes, review binding, one-time secrets                       |\n| DLP/deployment catalogue               | C08,C10         | Exact supported operation subset and real multipart semantics                        |\n| Sessions/content/search/cache          | C11             | Explicit reveal, protected persistence, bounded completeness                         |\n| Metadata FP/FN reports                 | C11             | Edit/remove/undo/export without suppression side effects                             |\n| Home/Inbox/source observations/charts  | C12,C18         | Runtime and Gateway data, freshness/partial/unknown/attention                        |\n| Five-product Library/specifications    | C12             | Authoring/import/export; only supported Runtime execution                            |\n| Red Team read-only observations        | C13             | Accepted actual reads; no invented live execution                                    |\n| Model/skill read-only observations     | C13             | Accepted actual reads and partial-source disclosure                                  |\n| API Client documents/projects/imports  | C14             | Fresh-input reconciliation, drafts/conflicts/secrets/Git adoption                    |\n| API Client transport/history/catalogue | C15             | Real HTTP/cookies/downloads, scoped auth, no mutation bypass                         |\n| Gateway workspace/config/admin subset  | C16,C17         | Supported scope/options and reviewed multi-step outcomes                             |\n| Gateway inference/logs/telemetry/cost  | C18             | Streaming, route/model selection, operational budgets/limits                         |\n| Harness native run/stream/lifecycle    | C19             | Real Rust engine/protocol, cleanup and no packaged Node runtime                      |\n| Harness identity/protected history     | C20             | Rust SSO/key lifecycle, scope-safe restore/export/delete                             |\n| Harness typed tools/project permission | C21             | Investigation/proposals and enforced existing sandbox restrictions                   |\n| Workspace MCP                          | C22             | Rust auth/grants, exact call approvals and execution evidence                        |\n| Academy identity/account isolation     | C04             | Device OAuth, scope admission, tenant-independent learner identity                   |\n| Academy learner presentation           | C05             | Shared catalog/lessons/checks/sandbox/UI; feature-local gamification                 |\n| Academy Program/exams/certificates     | C06             | Hosted access/timing/grading/award authority and account workflows                   |\n| Academy encrypted offline work         | C07             | Lease/revision/account-aware reading/drafts; explicit submission                     |\n| Reviewed imports/fresh authentication  | C23             | User work, source preservation, restart/rollback; no old approvals                   |\n| Native UX/privacy/measured performance | C01–C24         | Per-chunk checks plus cumulative actual native quality closure                       |\n| Controlled live behavior               | C25             | Designated scope, real outcomes, readback/cleanup, honest gaps                       |\n| Signed installed package/update        | C26             | DMG/notary/Gatekeeper, exact bytes, fresh user, coexistence/rollback                 |\n\n## Required per-workflow ledger\n\nThe [workflow ledger](../architecture/workflows.md) expands these families into\n66 operation/workflow classes. C01 records machine-readable evidence rows and\nconsuming chunks map exact supported operations/options before implementation.\nEvery row needs an ID, baseline source, current classification, responsible chunk,\nexact SDK/shared dependency contract, and independent expectations. Preserve four\nevidence categories separately: source implementation, fixture/contract results,\nnative/live qualification, and final installed-artifact qualification. Record\ndenied/unsupported/partial results honestly rather than collapsing them to pass.\n\nG01/C01 adds a live_requirement (required/not_required with reason) and separate\nready/blocked-by-access status to every row under [live-checks.md](live-checks.md).\nThe owner approves the expanded list before C25. Missing access cannot shrink\nmandatory scope; a reduction requires a recorded owner decision and new review.\nT23 cross-feature offline acceptance applies to local work and pending operations,\nnot just Academy. C24 closes that cumulative matrix.\n\nV2 source completeness alone does not close a row. Each workflow needs applicable\nsuccess and failure assertions, context/authority coverage, native acceptance and\nrequired live/artifact evidence. C25/C26 review the cumulative ledger against\nthe final frozen input set. A required blocked check blocks stable completion.\n\n## Intentional non-goals and retained classifications\n\n- Financial desktop billing/monetization and media-generation infrastructure are\n  excluded. Remote operational cost/limit administration is retained.\n- Exception Patterns remains explicitly synthetic/inert if shown. FP/FN reports\n  do not suppress scans or modify policy.\n- New Red Team execution and dedicated Model Security execution remain outside\n  parity. Their existence in the new SDK does not authorize adding product scope.\n- Academy admin/assessor operations remain web-only. Learner feedback with an\n  administrative namespace is classified by actual authority, not string matching.\n- No browser app, in-app updater, or additional supported OS/architecture is\n  required for first stable v2.\n- Shared Academy appearance replaces the GPUI presentation implementation. Do\n  not infer a pixel clone or identical internal widget/theme catalogue. Preserve\n  theme preference workflows and specify reviewed mappings for imported settings.\n- The generic SCM workbench catalogue does not prove all 193 requests were live\n  tested. Classify its supported execution/admission separately from dedicated\n  workflows and bind its request metadata to a reviewed source snapshot.\n\nManaged scan/identity HTTP deliberately defaults to Direct rather than v1's\nambient system proxy behavior. On mandatory-proxy networks, onboarding/settings\nshows Direct/System/Explicit policy and requires operator selection; a failed\nfirst request must surface proxy setup rather than an unexplained regression.\nGeneric workbench remains System by default. C02/C15 qualify all modes.\n# Provenance and intentional deviations\n\nEvery fixture file and screen contract cites the frozen prototype file and the\nprototype state names it derives from. This file lists where the\nimplementation deliberately differs from the prototype and why. Anything not\nlisted here is expected to match the prototype's behaviour and spacing.\n\nFrozen reference: canvas https://claude.ai/artifact/2SEXT1XMHdZkxHvdw8oaYL,\npage \"Refined Native v2\", version 134 (version id 1793059808-4929, published\n2026-10-26). Files and hashes: [prototype/MANIFEST.sha256](prototype/MANIFEST.sha256).\n\n| # | Prototype | Implementation | Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | Wizard tenant step draws the scan key, management client secret and Gateway workspace key as text fields (`R2Onboard` `f.scanKey`, `f.secret`, `f.gwKey`) | \"Add scan key…\" button opens the host's native secret sheet; the step then shows a masked indicator and the vault record id | The web layer never holds a secret (D014, C09 reveal ownership). Visual delta: button plus masked state instead of a field |\n| P2 | Rotation panel renders the secret in a readonly input with Reveal/Copy (`R2App` `secretShown`) | Reveal shows the secret in the host secret sheet; Copy goes through the host clipboard; both consume a one-time disclosure token | Invariant I6; same reason as P1 |\n| P3 | State lives in one component with `byTenant` (`R2App` `TS/ts/tPatch`) | Core-owned scopes per [state-ownership.md](state-ownership.md) | D014 authority; prototype state model is normative for invariants only |\n| P4 | ⌘1–⌘9 are advertised but not bound (canvas rule: no global key handlers) | Native menu defines the shortcuts and dispatches `navigate` | Host owns menus; works under VoiceOver and shows in the menu bar |\n| P5 | Sidebar lists Home, Inbox, Harness, Academy, Test Cases, API Client, Administration | Same, backed by Rust feature ids `home`, `inbox`, `harness`, `academy`, `library`, `apiClient`, `administration`; `gateway` and `redTeam` move inside Administration; `sessions`, `import`, `settings` reachable from the palette and Administration | One Rust inventory feeds menu, sidebar and palette |\n| P6 | Wizard boards are 1440×900 with a fixed 320px rail | Minimum window 1180×720; rail collapses to icons below 1280 wide | 13-inch displays |\n| P7 | Toasts expire on timers always | Persist until dismissed under Reduce Motion | Native acceptance Reduce Motion check |\n| P8 | Inbox items, Harness lifecycles, API responses, cases and Academy progress are invented in-component data | Wire fixtures in `packages/desktop-contracts/fixtures/wire`, content derived from the prototype data | Fixtures are the contract test surface; prototype data is never shipped |\n| P9 | `TOPICS.injo` added in round 16 as a separate key from detector `inj` | Core topic inventory keys are a distinct type from detector ids | Carries the round-16 fix into the type system |\n| P10 | Administration creation label is a per-product single value | Per-tab creation capability and label in `AdminInventory` | Round 16 finding: wrong label on API keys, Security profiles and Logs tabs |\n| P11 | Home snooze button hard-codes \"Snooze 24 hours\" | Item-specific `snoozeLabel` shared by Home and Inbox | Round 16 finding; invariant I11 |\n| P12 | API Client Send has no live completion status | Compact `role=\"status\"` region with request name, response code and duration; JSON body outside the live region | Round 16 finding |\n| P13 | Tenant switch with an open applied key review leaves disclosure open | Any review exit, including switch, finalises disclosure | Round 16 finding; invariant I6 |\n| P14 | Applied injection-override examples show \"Not demonstrated\" in the Playground | Topic examples classify under the topic key with a profile-aware verdict | Round 16 finding |\n| P15 | Import from v1 is a separate destination | Also offered on the wizard tenant step as a secondary action; credentials re-entered via the secret sheet | First run is where an upgrading user needs it |\n| P16 | Harness lifecycle 2 (mcp) reads an AES endpoint inventory (`CONVOS.mcp.tool` = `aes-inventory/get_endpoints`) and its follow-up is an AES policy review | The fixture reads the workspace MCP server inventory the core already exposes (`GatewayRead::McpServers`, C22) and the follow-up opens a Gateway guardrail review (`ChangeKind::GatewayGuardrails`) | AES has no admitted service (P18); the lifecycle stays read-only as in the prototype |\n| P17 | Wizard tenant step requires a management OAuth client id and secret (`ERR.clientId`, `ERR.secret`) and makes the scan key optional (`scanOn`) | First run requires the scan key and makes the management client an optional second section; add mode keeps both optional. Secrets enter only through the host secret sheet | D013 (scan-key-first; management credentials are separate and never imply scan authentication) |\n| P18 | Six products are verified, inventoried and administered (AIS tool policies, AES discovered AI and policies, AES policy review `REVIEWS.aes`) | Only products with an admitted service are verified, inventoried or changed: Runtime Security (`ManagementResource`, `ChangeKind`), Gateway (`GatewayRead`, Gateway `ChangeKind`s), Red Team and Model/skill reads (C13, read-only). Agentic Identity Security and Agentic Endpoint Security render as \"Not available in this release\" with their dependency gate; no inventory, verification result or mutation is invented for them | The existing interfaces do not supply those product APIs; fixtures cannot establish a capability (parity matrix, C13 rule) |\n| P19 | Wizard Academy step creates a local account with streaks, reminders and a quiz (`s.acad`, `s.af`, `s.streaks`, `s.remind`, `s.quiz`) | The step signs in to the hosted Academy identity or skips; the embedded learner view owns progress and gamification | D004–D006, C04 |\n| P20 | Key rotation issues a new key with a 24-hour grace period, a later revocation and a secret recoverable from the Keychain (`REVIEWS.key`, `keyRotated/keySecretClosed/keyDeployed/keyDoneAt`) | Two admitted operations in sequence: `ApiKeys · Version` (additional key, one-time reveal through the existing `RevealSecret` host sheet, never persisted) → local deployment acknowledgement → `ApiKeys · Delete` of the old key with readback. Two distinct timestamps; a lost secret means \"issue another key\" | `changes.rs` 842–846 and `management.rs` 980–988 define Rotate (regenerate, no grace) and Version (additional key); DESIGN.md \"Secrets\" forbids persisting issued keys |\n| P21 | Test Cases \"Run\" was described in the first spec draft as scanning any case | Runtime-only \"Open in Playground\"; \"Inspect\" for every other product, which never dispatches a scan | Prototype lines 1035–1037 and the parity matrix (no invented execution) |\n| P22 | Red Team Targets tab lists target names (`rtm` tables) | Targets shows `target_id` and `target_type` only | The C13 contract (`red_team.rs` 5–8, 56–66) deliberately keeps target names out of the core's reads; the privacy boundary is not widened for presentation |\n| P23 | One rotation flow for every key | Staged Version → Delete rotation for Runtime API keys only; Gateway keys use `Rotate` (regenerate in place) | `gateway_changes::allowed` admits Rotate, not Version, for Gateway keys |\n| P24 | Minimum window 1180×720 (DESIGN-V2 §4, tokens.md) | The host keeps `min_inner_size(900, 600)` until C34 because C01-N09 (re-run cumulatively by C24-N06) pins the legacy minimum; the v2 shell is laid out to stay usable without horizontal scroll at 900×600 (rail collapsed to icons below 1280 CSS px) and C28-N07 asserts it at that size. C34 raises the minimum together with the legacy-script retirement | Legacy native acceptance must keep passing while both shells exist (D058 §5) |\n| P25 | Revision of P1/P2: \"secrets enter only through a host secret sheet\" | The retained, reviewed entry path (C02/C09) is a webview password field read through a ref, cleared immediately and sent once inside the intent as `EnteredSecret` (zeroized, never in React state, never logged); the host owns the *reveal* sheet (`RevealSecret`) and the clipboard. C29 uses exactly this path for the scan key, management secret and Gateway key; no new host entry sheet is built | The C27 spec assumed an entry sheet the host does not have; the reviewed pattern already satisfies D014 and the C24 canary checks (no secret in any AX tree or file) |\n| P26 | `verifyTenantSetup` as one core intent streaming `VerifyStep`s (wizard.md) | Implemented as specified: `verifyTenantSetup{context}` runs in the core over the draft, admits the reads it needs through the management pipeline (management verify; the chosen Gateway workspace's prerequisites, or only the listing when none is chosen, reported as incomplete coverage; Red Team; model/skill) and settles each line from the result; AIS/AES are listed as not available and never read; the lines, `done`, `failed`, `blocking` and `stale` stream through `ShellSnapshot.setup.verify`. The scan key has no admitted read: its line is `stored`, never `pass`. Only the scan key (first run) blocks; an optional service's failure is carried as a warning the operator may continue over | Attempt-01 composed the reads in the presentation; attempt-02 read only the workspace listing and blocked on every failure — both rejected |\n| P27 | Add-tenant Cancel writes nothing except an already-vaulted credential | The wizard works on a core-owned draft (`beginTenantSetup`) with its own context and its own Gateway listing and choice; credentials attach to the draft; nothing is listed as a tenant (a draft id is refused by `selectTenant`/`deleteTenant`), the active tenant and its Gateway choice are untouched until `completeTenantSetup` commits the draft in one step and switches once. Every change to the draft's credentials or profile retires its context (and the binding, reads and listing under it). Cancel (`discardTenantSetup`) drops the draft, retires the binding and every read under its context and workspace reference, and queues its Keychain records for cleanup; the sheet closes only once the core admits it (a creation still pending or unconfirmed is awaited and whatever it produced is discarded). A relaunch, or a tenant committed outside the wizard during first run, does the same | Stronger than the spec's leftover credential: no orphan secrets and no cached authority surviving a replaced credential |\n| P28 | Gateway workspace key entered on the wizard tenant step | Implemented: an optional section reads the workspaces through the management client (`management` gateway inventory on the draft context), `selectGatewayWorkspace` chooses one, and `configureInferenceCredential` saves the endpoint and key for it | The admitted sequence (list → select → key) runs on the draft like on a tenant |\n| P29 | \"Import from v1\" fills the tenant step in place | The action navigates to the reviewed import destination (C23), which renders on its own during first run with a 'Back to setup' control; the wizard resumes afterwards | The import review flow is itself reviewed and must not be duplicated inside the wizard |\n| P30 | The wizard opens whenever onboarding is not done | The shell shows onboarding as done whenever a tenant already exists (an upgrade, or a tenant created before the v2 shell was chosen); the stored state is untouched. A first run is one with no tenant yet; a relaunch mid-setup reopens at the Tenant step with a fresh draft (the previous draft's credentials are cleaned up) | Existing users must never be sent through the wizard; a half-made draft is never a tenant |\n| P31 | Products step shows licence state read from the tenant | No admitted service reports licensing (DESIGN-V2 §6); each card shows what it reads with (scan key / management client, saved or not) and, once verified, that product's verify result; a note says licence state is not readable through the public APIs | Nothing may be inferred about licensing from reads that do not report it |\n| P32 | First-run import returns to the wizard | A v1 import during first run commits its tenants through the reviewed C23 flow; the desktop then has a tenant, so the first-run draft ends (its credentials are cleaned up) and the shell opens on the imported tenant; its scan key is added in Administration (the reviewed C08/C09 credential setup) | A tenant that exists is never sent back through a first-run wizard (P30), and the wizard cannot wrap an existing tenant without duplicating it |\n| P33 | `ReadStatus` as its own core scope with per-product states from dedicated reads | `readTenant` admits one finite read per chosen product through the existing management pipeline: the Runtime overview the shell already owns (sessions chart, key inventory, the chosen Gateway workspace's usage and limits), the Red Team statistics and scan list (C13's read), the model scans, skill statistics and skill scans (C13's read); each settles into the tenant's read record as a per-source outcome (read, needs a role, failed); a source counts as read only when it established what it is for: a listing whose page is the inventory (not capped, nothing beyond the first page), a sessions chart with consistent totals, a key page with every expiration reported, Gateway usage with the day's requests and errors both known; anything less is named in `sourcesMissing` (the same gaps the Inbox shows). A product attempt that established nothing (every source failed, or the read never reached the service) is kept as the latest failed attempt beside the product's last outcome that did, which stays shown as older; the read is failed while such an attempt is the latest (a missing role is a settled attempt reported on the product, not a failed read). Once anything was attempted, every chosen source nothing has reached yet is named as unread coverage too, and `settledAt` carries the time of the latest attempt so a completion that established nothing is announced for when it settled, never as a first read still pending. A healthy empty read older than the ten-minute window (`OverviewView.fresh`, C12) is a gap: Home and the Inbox keep the cached read and name its age instead of an all-clear, never re-reading on their own; a refresh in progress keeps that read and its age named until the new read lands (admission restores nothing). A chosen Gateway with no workspace is unread coverage, named in `sourcesMissing`; `recheckProduct` re-reads one chosen product. The read completes (one generation) when every part admitted under the tenant's current context has landed; a read in which no source answered is a failed read (`failed`, with `failedAt`), never pending and never done, and a failed refresh keeps the earlier read labelled as older | No second read pipeline, and Home never claims coverage a read did not establish (I4): revised after the C30 attempt-01 review, which found selected Red Team and model/skill products left unread and a workspace-less Gateway announced as complete |\n| P34 | Inbox items with `kind`, `severity` and `explanation` per item | `InboxEntry` carries the overview item (Runtime, Gateway) or the C29 setup warning with its hold (listed, with every action, whatever the read status: a warning exists before any read); the explanation is per kind in the presentation's copy, not invented per item; `deadline` is absent (P20). Its action carries an `InboxTarget` (destination, product, Administration inventory, item name): a key item opens Administration on the API keys inventory filtered to the key, a setup warning opens where it is fixed (Settings for the scan key or management client, AI Gateway, AI Red Teaming) | The admitted reads report no per-item explanation or deadline; the target was added after the attempt-01 review found actions landing on the destination's default view |\n| P35 | Guard offers \"Archive and switch\" / \"Discard and switch\" | Archive ends the conversation (its protected history stays as C20 saved it); Discard ends it, removes its protected history record (after any save under way, under the same lock the sink holds) and refuses every later save of it (the turn the stop cuts short), then drops it once its task returns; both then switch once; the pending approval is never decided for the operator; the dialog's initial focus is Stay, the safe default (Enter on arrival ends nothing). A save admitted before the discard (the turn the engine's end cut short) re-checks the tombstone under the history hold before it writes, so it can never write the discarded transcript back after the removal. A removal that fails (storage unavailable, access denied) is never implied to have succeeded: the record stays listed and the workspace's history reports the failure, through unrelated opens and removals, until the operator removes the record | The engine is stopped either way: a switch retires its binding (C20); the difference is whether the transcript is kept, which the dialog promises and the attempt-01 review found unkept |\n| P36 | Completing \"Add a tenant\" switches to the new tenant | `completeTenantSetup{resolution?}` goes through the same guard as `selectTenant`: a pending Harness decision refuses it with `switchBlocked{to: <draft id>, completion: true}`, nothing is committed or retired, the draft stays, and the wizard renders the one guard dialog and re-sends the completion with the resolution | Wizard completion is listed among the entry points of the single guarded switch path (shell.md, I1); the attempt-01 review found it bypassing the guard |\n| P37 | Scopes are per tenant and a switch round trip shows the cached state | Each opened tenant owns its read record in the core (its last overview, the other products' outcomes, generation, recheck, snooze receipt) for the session; a switch away and back shows it again without a new read. Per-operation bookkeeping is separate from the cached data: each read part is tracked with the context it was admitted under, \"reading\" means a part under the current context is open, a part under a retired context is withdrawn when it lands (never recorded, never counted), so a read held open across a switch round trip can never leave the tenant reading. The presentation's own transient state follows the tenant too: the shell store carries the shown scope (set from each snapshot before it renders); a toast for a tenant's item or read and an unconfirmed delivery bound to a context are refused at insertion when that scope is no longer shown (a refusal or lost delivery arriving after a switch), retired on leaving it (however long Reduce Motion would keep them) and filtered at render; a contextual arrival belongs to the tenant it was followed in | I2 names the tenant, not the context, as the owner of its cached scopes; the attempt-01 review found the overview bound to the context and lost across A → B → A |\n| P38 | Snooze toast states the core-computed return time | The core issues a `SnoozeReceipt{id, title, returnsAt, sequence}` on the tenant's Inbox scope for the last snooze placed in the session; the shell announces a new sequence once, in the tenant it was placed in, and never a hold a switch or a reread merely rediscovers; a snooze ends at its return time whether or not its entry is actionable, and the core's deadline ticker publishes the expiry (the holds in effect are part of the overview's time signature) | I11 wants one source for the return time and one announcement per snooze; diffing holds between snapshots announced old holds after a switch (attempt-01 review) |\n| P39 | `AdminInventory` generated from screens/administration-inventory.md | Implemented as a Rust table (`admin_inventory::inventory`) carried on `ShellSnapshot.administration` in the v2 shell only (null in the legacy shell, whose literal snapshot and leak canary are unchanged; the presentation's fixture copy is pinned to the table by a core test); a unit test and the `ui_v2_reviews` suite pin every offered (kind, action) to `changes::is_allowed` and every denied pair to its refusal, so a label can never outrun the core. Read and Change tabs render the retained views (the same reads and reviewed changes) embedded under the accordion with the inventory's labels; Unavailable tabs and products show their gate and nothing else; Removed tabs are not rendered. The Gateway MCP servers tab lists the chosen workspace's catalog through `listMcpServers` | One source of truth for dispositions, checked against the matrices the core enforces (V2-T20, V2-R11) |\n| P40 | `openReview`/`confirmReview{typed}` intents | The retained review intents are the review (`reviewChange` binds tenant, context, credentials and the read baseline; `requestApply{confirmation}` validates the typed name in the core and opens the native confirmation, whose private completion alone applies). The v2 sheet disables Apply until the typed text equals the review's name and renders the applied panel only from the core's result and readback | The C09 review already carries every binding I3 asks for; new intents would duplicate it |\n| P41 | Rotation state per tenant with two stored timestamps | `Tenant.rotations` (persisted, never a secret): driven from settled `ApiKeys` results of the active context (a Version read back starts one; an unknown Version starts an unconfirmed one; a failed one records nothing; the old key's Delete read back absent completes it; a rejected Delete keeps it with the reason; an unknown one is reconciled by a complete key inventory read), plus `acknowledgeDeployment{application}` stored once. The reminder is the Inbox item `rotation-<application>` while a key is confirmed and the old one not read back gone. The one-time secret stays on its change result for the context it was applied in: Reveal is offered only while the core holds it; any sheet exit forgets it and a context change drops it | The issued secret must never be persisted (I6) while the stage and timestamps must survive reads, switches and relaunches (I7) |\n| P42 | Playground topic examples classify under the topic | The scan result carries `topics{violation, blocked, allowed}` from the service's `topic_violation` flag and `topic_guardrails_details`, shown apart from the detector flags: a matched topic is \"flagged (blocked)\" or \"flagged (monitor only)\", no match under the profile is \"Not detected\", an unreported verdict says so; detectors stay listed beside it. The prototype's local classifier is not carried: verdicts come from the service | The desktop never invents a verdict (DESIGN-V2 §10 gap 2 closed with service data) |\n| P43 | C31 live row runs through `xtask live-run` | `live-run --family L03 --subject C31` runs the C31 topic routine inside L03's designation and writes its receipts and run record under evidence/C31/live, leaving C25's receipts untouched (the consumption ledger stays C25's single ledger) | The live row needs the reviewed runner and the owner's designation, not a second harness |\n| P44 | Lifecycle conversations are seeded data in the prototype (`CONVOS`) | The three lifecycles are fixtures in the contracts package (`fixtures/wire/harness-lifecycle-{blocks,mcp,keys}.json`), one `ConversationView` per step (asking, approved, declined, ended) plus the saved transcript. Each read is one of the desktop's own read tools (`desktopRead`, `effect: readOnly`): blocks `search`, mcp `mcpServers`, keys `inventory` of API keys. They are regression inputs and boards only: a real tenant never shows them (I12) | The core decides approvals; fixtures must be shapes the core emits, validated by `parseShellSnapshot` |\n| P45 | The mcp and keys follow-ups open a guardrail review and the key rotation flow | `proposeChange` admits, besides the Runtime and DLP kinds, an API key `version` (the hand-off of a rotation: it opens the C31 review sheet and its staged sequence) and a guardrail of the conversation's own Gateway workspace (create or update only), staged with that workspace's reference so its review is bound to it; Gateway deletes and every other Gateway change, and a key rotation in place, stay refused. The follow-up of a key hand-off shows the rotation stage from `ShellSnapshot.rotations`. The mcp lifecycle's guardrail is described as what it is (a pii-check guardrail on all messages while the unlisted server is reviewed); no guardrail expresses an MCP allow list | A proposal only ever opens the operator's own review (D014); the follow-ups must be core-owned, not page-invented |\n| P46 | `endConversation` returns an archive id | `endHarness` keeps its receipt; the ended conversation names its saved transcript (`ConversationView.historyId`, the protected history record, shown only with save evidence: `savedAt` set and no `saveFailure`) and the rail marks it ended; \"Open the saved transcript\" opens that record | The archive already exists as the C20 protected history; a second id would duplicate it |\n| P47 | The mcp lifecycle reads `GatewayRead::McpServers` (P16) | A new desktop read tool `mcpServers` reads the conversation workspace's MCP catalog (`GatewayRead::McpServers`, the read the MCP panel uses) for the engine, decided in the page like every desktop read | The prototype's AES inventory has no admitted service; the Gateway catalog is the admitted read P16 named |\n| P48 | Read-only tool calls are approved in the page (C21 assumed) | Every desktop read tool (inventory, detail, session, search, Red Teaming, model observation, scan, test cases, MCP catalog) now waits for the operator's decision in the page (`ApprovalKind::DesktopRead`, `effect: readOnly`); declined, nothing is read and the engine is told so. Staging a proposal reads nothing and still runs at once. Before C32 these reads ran without asking | The lifecycles' approve and decline paths, and the operator's control over what tenant data reaches the model |\n| P49 | The core decides from the tool's write flag whether the native confirmation is required (C21 assumed) | No such branch existed: every decision was in-page. The core now classifies each decision (`HarnessApproval.effect`): a desktop read, or a command while the project is read-only, is `readOnly`; a file change, a command with changes allowed and any workspace MCP tool (whose effect the desktop cannot know; tools carry no trusted annotations) is `mayChange`, and allowing it opens the native confirmation (`Effect::ConfirmHarness`), whose private completion alone sends the allow; refused, the decision stays pending; a stale one is dismissed. In TypeScript `effect` is optional only so pre-C32 snapshots type-check, and absent is treated as `mayChange` | In-page consent must never authorise a change (D058, C32) |\n| P50 | A composer draft per conversation | One composer per tenant and workspace (by the stable workspace ID): its draft survives navigation, tenant round trips and the retirement of the conversation it was typed for. A message the core did not take is kept under \"Not sent\"; one whose delivery was not confirmed under \"Not confirmed\". Each send carries the presentation's own ID (`sendHarness.submission`, 32 hex): the core takes each ID at most once (a repeated delivery after a lost receipt sends nothing again) and reports the sends it took with the message each became and where each stands (`ConversationView.submissions`, newest 64): `pending` (taken, a sign-in renewal or the write confirmation under way: it may still be given back), `delivered` (its message entered the transcript, recorded by the engine session apart from the transcript's bounds) or `returned` (given back, or failed without an engine). Only `delivered` and `returned` settle anything; a tracked send whose conversation is no longer shown before its final word (retired, cleared, another tenant or workspace meanwhile) is kept as Not confirmed with its text. A \"Not confirmed\" record is settled only by its own conversation reporting its own ID: delivered, it leaves the list (and the box, if it holds exactly that text); given back, it leaves the list and its exact text stays in the box or is kept under Not sent, never cleared; an admitted send is likewise tracked until its conversation reports it, and its receipt never clears text the core already gave back, whichever arrives first; never by matching text, another send's entry, another conversation, or the transcript (which drops its oldest entries past 200 entries or 1 MiB). The core numbers a conversation's messages and never reuses an ID, also when restored. Nothing is sent by itself | A conversation's draft must stay recoverable after the core retires the conversation (C32 reviews) |\n| P51 | A new investigation prefilled with the ended conversation's opening question | The rail titles the current conversation from its kept opening too, so its title never changes as the transcript drops its oldest entries. A contextual arrival stays pending, bound to its tenant and workspace, until the conversation or the history resolves it (\"Finding that investigation…\" while the saved conversations are listed); listed without it, or not listable, the page says so; navigation, a new arrival, another workspace or a restart cancels it. The opening question is the conversation's opening message, whole, kept by the core apart from its title (a one-line, shortened label for lists): `ConversationView.opening` and `OpenedConversationView.opening`, captured when the first message (`u-1`) enters the transcript, saved with the record, restored with it and kept after the transcript drops its oldest entries. An archived conversation's record is read for it when the restart is asked for (nothing is filled in meanwhile); choosing another investigation, sending, opening a transcript, a new arrival or another workspace retires that read (a late result changes nothing); otherwise it settles by the core's receipt for the record (`HarnessHistoryView.lastOpen`: id, admission order, failure), newer than any open settled before it was asked for, never by a busy edge or a changed error. When it is not known (a record saved before C32 whose transcript no longer holds its first message) or the record cannot be read, the page says so and never fills in the title instead; a draft in the box is kept under Not sent and nothing is sent | The title is shortened (72 or 80 characters, one line) and lost the rest of a long or multi-line question; the attempt-12 review found the restart prefilling it |\n| P52 | The API Client re-skinned to the prototype's layout (request tree, tabs, method/URL line, 40% response pane) | The v2 API Client presents the C14/C15 workbench in the v2 layout through a layout context its wrapper provides (`shell/presentation.ts` `V2Layout`): the page heading becomes the v2 title row (no kicker), and the request editor puts the method, URL, Send and Save on one compact line with the request details beside a response pane (stacked when narrow). Collections, tabs, history, reviews and execution are the existing components and authority, unchanged; the legacy shell keeps its layout. Send's completion is announced in the editor's status region as \"<request> · <code> · <duration>\", the body outside it, in the response pane (known gap 5, V2-T14). The collection tree and tab strip keep the workbench's own arrangement | The workbench view itself is held by uncommitted owner work in this tree and is not edited; its layout is changed only through the components it renders |\n| P53 | \"Generic requests … refused by the core as today\" (screens/api-client-test-cases.md) | Unchanged C15 authority: a request to a known tenant operation that would change configuration needs its exact, natively confirmed review (`ReviewRequired`) before it is sent; an unknown operation at a registered origin is refused (`UnknownEndpoint`) | The screen text summarised both as refused; the core's existing rule is the authority and C33 does not change it |\n| P54 | Show the calls reveals each action's request and response \"as the diagnostics module already redacts them\" | The diagnostics module records typed events only. The core now records, in memory for the app run, each call it makes on a tenant's behalf through its transport (scans, management reads and changes with their token requests, AI Gateway inference): purpose, the credential by kind (the core attaches it), method, origin and path without the query (a query is marked withheld), header names (never values), request size, status or failure, duration and response content type, newest 50 per tenant (`ShellSnapshot.calls`, only while the preference is on; forgotten with the tenant), bounded as the contract accepts them (header names of at most 64 characters, at most 128, path cut at 2048 characters, method of at most 16, status 100-999, HTTPS origins only; `CALL_*_LIMIT`). Bodies are never kept: a response can carry an issued key or a token and requests carry tenant content. The API Client's own requests keep their existing redacted history. The preference is the operator's once chosen (`show_calls_chosen`), otherwise on until onboarding completes | The prototype has no calls panel; DESIGN-V2 §3 asked for it; secrets must never reach a snapshot (I6) |\n| P55 | Test Cases with per-case results and pack names (prototype `results`, `packCases`) | A case carries its starter pack's name (set when the bundled catalog's cases are added, at onboarding completion or by Add starter packs) and its last result: the verdict of a Playground scan opened from the case, recorded by the core when the scan settles and only while the case still holds the prompt and response that were scanned (`TestCase.result`, `Intent::Scan.case`). Neither is ever exported or imported (v1's file format is unchanged); a copy to another tenant drops the result. A case can be named by a scan only if it is a Runtime Security case of the tenant's open library (otherwise the scan is refused before anything is sent); a result stored on any other case is dropped when the library is read. Absent result: Not run. The library's workflows are kept in the v2 screen: New case, Add starter packs, Add public examples, Import…, Export…, Remove and explicit Copy to another tenant, with the library's unsaved, failure, problem and notice states and their recovery (Save again, Open the library again). Search and the product filter run over name, pack and input; pages of 20 (at most 32 cases per tenant, v1's limit) | The library had no result or pack; the prototype's results were UI state |\n| P56 | \"Open in Playground\" sets the Playground's prompt, direction and profile | It hands the case's profile, prompt, response and application to Administration's Runtime Playground (the v2 home of the Runtime scan form) and opens that tab; the operator runs the scan there, and that scan names the case. The scan form has no separate direction: a case's prompt and response are its directions. Nothing is scanned by opening it, and Inspect dispatches nothing | The Runtime scan form (C03) is the admitted execution surface; direction is expressed by which fields hold text |\n\nP10–P14 are the five round-16 Codex items carried as known gaps (see\n[DESIGN-V2.md](DESIGN-V2.md) \"Known gaps\"). P16–P21 were added after the C27\nattempt-01 review (R3–R6); P22–P23 after attempt-03 (F3a, F3b); P24–P25 were recorded in C28 and P26–P32 in C29 and P33–P38 in C30 and P39–P43 in C31, P44–P51 in C32 and P52–P56 in C33 when implementation met the host as it is (P26–P28 revised after the C29 attempt-01 review; P26–P27 again, and P32 added, after attempt-02; P33–P35 revised and P36–P38 added after the C30 attempt-01 review).\n# Prototype boards → test fixtures\n\nEvery wrapper board on the canvas page \"Refined Native v2\" (version 134) is\na reviewed state. Each becomes a named Vitest fixture with the same initial\nprops, so reviewed-state coverage is auditable from this table without the\ncanvas. Props are the `data-props` of `prototype/R2App.dc.html`\n(`start`, `convo`, `theme`, `overlay`) and `prototype/R2Onboard.dc.html`\n(`step`, `filled`, `verify`, `theme`, `mode`).\n\n| Board | Component | Initial props | Fixture id | Primary tests | Chunk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R2App (default) | R2App | start=home, theme=light | `fx.home` | V2-T09, T10, T11 | C28 |\n| R2Dark | R2App | start=home, theme=dark | `fx.home.dark` | token contrast snapshot | C28 |\n| R2Palette | R2App | start=home, overlay=palette | `fx.palette` | palette filter/dispatch, N03 | C28 |\n| R2Tenants | R2App | start=home, overlay=tenants | `fx.switcher` | V2-T01, T02 | C28/C30 |\n| R2AddTenant | R2Onboard | mode=add, step=tenant | `fx.addTenant` | cancel semantics, no Back on the first step | C29 |\n| R2AddTenant drafted | R2Onboard | mode=add, step=tenant, drafted | `fx.addTenant.drafted` | optional scan key, Continue open | C29 |\n| R2On01 Welcome | R2Onboard | step=welcome | `fx.on.welcome` | copy constants | C29 |\n| R2On02 Tenant | R2Onboard | step=tenant, filled=false | `fx.on.tenant.empty` | validation, aria-invalid | C29 |\n| R2On02b Tenant filled | R2Onboard | step=tenant, filled=true | `fx.on.tenant.filled` | rail context line, continue enabled | C29 |\n| R2On03 Products | R2Onboard | step=products | `fx.on.products` | unavailable products not selectable (P18) | C29 |\n| R2On04 Verify | R2Onboard | step=verify, verify=running | `fx.on.verify.running` | streaming order | C29 |\n| R2On04b Verify warning | R2Onboard | step=verify, verify=warn | `fx.on.verify.warn` | warning row → Inbox item at completion | C29 |\n| R2On04c Verify fail | R2Onboard | step=verify, verify=fail | `fx.on.verify.fail` | fix-and-verify-again path | C29 |\n| R2On04d Verify stale | R2Onboard | step=verify, verify=done, inputs changed | `fx.on.verify.stale` | results no longer count; later steps unreachable | C29 |\n| R2On05 Packs | R2Onboard | step=packs | `fx.on.packs` | pack counts, optional | C29 |\n| R2On06 Academy | R2Onboard | step=academy | `fx.on.academy` | hosted sign-in or skip (P19) | C29 |\n| R2On07 Done | R2Onboard | step=done | `fx.on.done` | finite open, `onboarding=done` | C29 |\n| R2Inbox | R2App | start=inbox | `fx.inbox` | V2-T04, T12, T13 | C30 |\n| R2Fresh | R2App | start=home, tenant never read | `fx.fresh` | first read pending, empty Inbox (I12) | C30 |\n| R2Admin | R2App | start=admin | `fxAdmin` (v2/admin/administration.test.tsx) | V2-T19, T20, per-tab labels | C31 |\n| R2Review | R2App | start=admin, overlay=review | `fxAdmin` + `review()`/`rotation()` (same file) | V2-T03, T06, T08; T07 in `ui_v2_reviews` | C31 |\n| R2Harness | R2App | start=harness, convo=blocks | `fxHarness` + `harness-lifecycle-blocks.json` (v2/harness/harness.test.tsx) | V2-T17 | C32 |\n| R2Harness2 | R2App | start=harness, convo=mcp | `fxHarness` + `harness-lifecycle-mcp.json` (v2/harness/harness.test.tsx) | V2-T17 (Gateway MCP read, P16) | C32 |\n| R2Harness3 | R2App | start=harness, convo=keys | `fxHarness` + `harness-lifecycle-keys.json` (v2/harness/harness.test.tsx) | V2-T17, T18 | C32 |\n| R2Api | R2App | start=api | `fxApi` (v2/fixtures.ts; v2/workbench/workbench.test.tsx) | V2-T14; V2-R05, T05 in `ui_v2_workbench` | C33 |\n| R2Cases | R2App | start=cases | `fxCases` (v2/fixtures.ts; v2/workbench/workbench.test.tsx) | V2-T15, T16 | C33 |\n| Show the calls | R2App, any start (no prototype board) | `fxCalls` (v2/fixtures.ts; v2/workbench/workbench.test.tsx) | panel shown only while chosen, never a secret (P54) | C33 |\n| R2Academy | R2App | start=academy | `fx.academy` | embed mounts, theme | C34 |\n\nTest ids: `V2-Txx` Vitest (desktop app), `V2-Rxx` Rust integration\n(desktop-core), `V2-Nxx` native checks (`native-checks.json`). Registry of\nids: [../invariants.md](../invariants.md) plus the per-screen contracts.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '150,195p' docs/planning/decisions.md; sed -n '2391,2515p' docs/planning/decisions.md; cat docs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '150,195p' docs/planning/decisions.md; sed -n '2391,2515p' docs/planning/decisions.md; cat docs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"## D013 — Scan-key-first onboarding\n\nAccepted in interview round 4, Q13.\n\nThe first scan supports an explicit scanning API key without requiring management\nOAuth credentials. Support scan-only tenants. Management credentials are separate\nand added for administration workflows; a management registration does not\nimplicitly supply scan authentication. Additional scan credential modes must be\nspecified and tested when needed, not inferred from an SDK constructor.\n\n## D014 — Rust application authority\n\nAccepted in interview round 4, Q14.\n\nRust owns tenant context, credential access, request lifecycles, and permission\nto apply configuration changes. React presents drafts, reviews, and results.\nConfiguration review binds the tenant, credentials, resource, and draft; changed\ncontext invalidates it. Harness proposals enter the same operator review flow\nand cannot authorize Apply. Initially preserve the existing Harness project/tool\nrestrictions; expansion would require a separate explicit design decision.\n\n## D015 — Import work, reestablish authentication\n\nAccepted in interview round 4, Q15.\n\nImport selected user-authored work and settings, including saved cases, reports,\nAcademy drafts, API Client projects, and historical conversations. Require fresh\ncredential setup and sign-in. Regenerate disposable caches/downloads. Imported\nconversations are inspectable history until resumption compatibility is validated;\nold pending approvals and authentication sessions never transfer.\n\nReading protected source work for an explicitly selected import is distinct from\ntransferring its old authentication credentials. Define restartable import\nreceipts, version/account checks, rollback, and source-preservation tests.\n\n## D016 — Explicit offline behavior\n\nAccepted in interview round 4, Q16.\n\nOffline mode supports local drafts, saved work, history, and valid downloaded\nAcademy content. Online operations require explicit retry after reconnection.\nDo not silently replay configuration changes, scans, grading submissions, or\nother remote actions. Preserve unknown/uncertain outcomes rather than asserting\nthat cancellation or disconnection undid remote work.\n\n## D017 — Operational usage/cost administration\n## D058 — UI v2 \"Refined Native\": frozen prototype, P09 extraction plan\n\nAccepted by the owner on 2026-10-26 (\"I accept it as written\"), with authority to implement P09 end to end autonomously and to proceed with C28 while C26 remains in progress; every chunk is reviewed until it scores 9 in every dimension.\n\nThe owner explored fifteen interactive shell concepts on a Claude Design canvas\n(https://claude.ai/artifact/2SEXT1XMHdZkxHvdw8oaYL), selected \"Refined\nNative\", and iterated its prototype (R2App, R2Onboard, R2Harness ×3,\nR2Rationale) through sixteen independent Codex usability reviews (scores 6, 7,\n7, then 8 on rounds 4–16). The prototype is frozen at canvas version 134 and\ncopied verbatim into `docs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/` with a SHA-256\nmanifest; the canvas page is not changed again. Prototype data is invented and\nis never shipped.\n\nDecisions (grilling session, 55 questions, all recommendations accepted):\n\n1. Extraction is spec, then TypeScript presentation, then Rust contract, as\n   phase P09 chunks C27–C34 (see the chunk index). The first implementation\n   slice is the shell plus the first-run wizard and add-tenant sheet.\n2. The prototype's state model is normative for twelve named invariants\n   (`docs/architecture/ui-v2/invariants.md`) and illustrative for code shape.\n   Every prototype state field is assigned Rust-owned/observed or UI-transient\n   (`state-ownership.md`); nothing goes to browser storage.\n3. D014 holds: secrets enter only through the host secret sheet and vault;\n   an issued API key is disclosed only through the existing `RevealSecret`\n   host path, held for the active context and never persisted; key rotation\n   is the admitted sequence additional key (`Version`) → local deployment\n   acknowledgement → old key `Delete` with readback (no invented grace\n   period or revocation); typed-name confirmation is validated in the core;\n   tenant switch is refused by the core while a Harness decision is pending;\n   write-capable tool calls use the native confirmation sheet.\n3a. Only products with an admitted service are verified, inventoried or\n   changed (Runtime Security, Gateway, Red Team and Model/skill reads).\n   Agentic Identity Security and Agentic Endpoint Security render as \"Not\n   available in this release\" with a dependency gate; no inventory,\n   verification result or mutation is invented for them.\n4. Tokens live in a local `--v2-*` file; Academy variables stay for the\n   embedded learner view. One Rust navigation inventory feeds the native menu,\n   sidebar and palette; ⌘1–⌘9 are menu-defined. Feature ids gain `home` and\n   `inbox`.\n5. The new shell ships behind a Rust-owned `ui_variant` flag and the legacy\n   shell, flag and legacy acceptance scripts are removed in C34. The Academy\n   learner view is embedded unchanged.\n6. The wizard realises D013 (scan key first and only required credential);\n   v1 import is offered on the tenant step.\n7. Verification: Vitest fixtures named after every prototype wrapper board,\n   Rust tests per invariant, native acceptance extended with focus ring, live\n   regions, topmost modal and Reduce Motion persistence, screenshot evidence\n   at 100%/200% in both themes per chunk. Live calls only in C29 (verify,\n   Vulture, reads) and C31 (review apply, Redtail, prefixed) inside the scope\n   designated 2026-10-25. C27 is reviewed at 9/10 in every dimension and the\n   controller enforces it: a `docs_only` chunk has no native preparation or\n   acceptance and refuses owner acceptance at completion and at recognition;\n   C28–C34 follow D034.\n8. Minimum window 1180×720 with the rail collapsing below 1280 wide; toasts\n   persist under Reduce Motion; \"Show the calls\" panel shows diagnostics-\n   redacted wire data; English only in P09; chunks commit on main except C34.\n9. C27 proceeds in parallel with C26 (docs and controller registration only);\n   C28 waits for C26.\n\n\n## D059 — P09 re-cut: port the prototype, one gate per screen at 8\n\nAccepted by the owner on 2026-10-29 after a grilling session (23 questions, all\naccepted; `docs/planning/p09-recut.md`). C32 had taken twenty review rounds\nwhile the built v2 screens never matched the design: chunks had wrapped the\nlegacy feature components in v2 frames instead of porting the prototype.\n\n- The frozen prototype (D058, version 134) is the specification per screen; the\n  canvas is not consulted (it is unreachable from the implementing account).\n- A screen is done when it matches its board side by side at 1440×900 in both\n  themes with real core data, judged by the owner from composites kept under\n  the chunk's evidence; parity is structural (regions, widths, spacing, type\n  scale, tokens, component shapes), content differs, accessibility\n  corrections may deviate and are noted. Parity precedes review.\n- One heavy gate per chunk after that approval (rebuild, native acceptance,\n  the full fixture suite, the Codex review), accepted at 8 under D032/D034; the\n  phase review stays 9 and every chunk accepted at 8 is raised in C44.\n- v2 screens import no legacy feature view; the legacy files stay for the\n  legacy shell until C44 removes it. C33 was committed without a receipt (its\n  core stands; its two screens are superseded).\n- Chunks: C35 shell, C36 Home, C37 Inbox fix-up, C38 Harness, C39 Administration\n  (a: Runtime Security, b: other products), C40 Test Cases, C41 API Client,\n  C42 Academy (prototype chrome around the hosted embed), C43 Wizard re-port\n  over the existing state machine, C44 legacy removal on a branch and the P09\n  review. C34 is superseded. `Subject::LAST_CHUNK` is 44.\n\n## Directions explicitly requested before round 1\n\n- Fresh application implementation; the existing GPUI codebase is primarily a\n  reference point.\n- React presentation, Rust application implementation, and the new Rust SCM SDK.\n- Tauri was initially acceptable if needed and is now selected by D008.\n- cdot-framework informs the backend foundation through D008's selective reuse\n  and architecture direction; individual imports still require evaluation.\n- Plan first, architecture improvement review second, implementation and package\n  qualification after that.\n- Independent numeric review gates for testable chunks; earlier requirement:\n  scores below 9/10 require remediation and another actual review.\n\n## Interview exit and remaining evidence\n\nThe product/process frontier through Q23 is settled; D024/D025 subsequently\nsettle source terms and all four architecture candidates. Continue the requested\narchitecture work from those decisions. The prior extra blanket scope-confirmation\nrequest must not prevent already authorized design/research. G01 still requires\nconcrete interfaces/commands and independent review before implementation.\nExact identity strings, dependency pins, import compatibility, named numeric\nbudgets, and designated live environments require explicit evidence/specification\nat their declared gates. They are not secretly assumed complete or reasons to\nrepeat settled product questions. No application implementation has started.\n\n## D060 — No import from the alpha; this desktop is v1\n\nDecided by the owner on 2026-10-31, in the owner's words: \"i really really really don't want to have an import from v1. in fact, i plan on calling this v1 since the previous was an alpha.\"\n\n- The previous desktop (`/Users/cdot/development/cdot65/prisma-airs-desktop`) is the alpha. Nothing is imported from it: the reviewed import of alpha work (C23: the Import from v1 destination and view, the wizard's \"Used Prisma AIRS Desktop 1?\" box and the tenant step's import link, the first-run import frame) is removed from the one presentation in C44, its native checks C23-N01..N09 are retired without replacement, and the legacy-script map and the C44 cumulative set drop `acceptance_c23.py`.\n- Still to decide the same way, in a follow-up chunk the owner names (proposed C45): the core's alpha-import intents and reader (`import_v1_domain`, the `V1Import*` intents, the host adapters), Settings' file-based \"Import tenant profiles\" and the API Client's \"Import v1 API Client profile\" adoption, which are alpha-import surfaces too; their removal changes contracts and earlier chunks' tests and is not done inside the P09 closure.\n- Naming: this desktop becomes v1 and the previous one the alpha. The rename touches the application identity that lets the two coexist (bundle identifiers, data roots, Keychain namespaces, D001-era identities) and the whole document set; it is its own chunk after P09, not a find-and-replace.\n- The product name shown today stays \"Prisma AIRS Desktop v2\" until that chunk.\n\n# Prisma AIRS Desktop v2 implementation plan\n\nStatus: product interview decisions D001–D023 accepted; D024/D025 settle source\nterms and all four architecture candidates; D026 makes Codex the independent\nchunk and phase reviewer. G00/G01 passed; implementation proceeds from C01. A passing plan review is not a passing architecture,\nSDK, application, live-tenant, or package review.\n\n## Outcome and exclusions\n\nDeliver a fresh macOS Apple Silicon desktop application with React presentation\nin Tauri, a focused Rust application core, direct Rust SCM SDK integration, and\nthe native Rust Harness engine. Academy supplies shared presentation and remains\nthe authority for remote learning. Preserve agreed working v1 behavior, using\nv1 as requirements reference. Do not translate its GPUI implementation wholesale.\n\nNo desktop billing, media-generation backend, local MySQL/Redis server stack,\nNode/Bun backend runtime, TypeScript workers, speculative browser client, or\nin-app updater is included. Academy gamification remains inside Academy. Gateway\nusage/cost/budget/limits remain operational product functionality. Build-time\nJavaScript tooling and React WebView JavaScript are expected.\n\nSource destination: `ssh://git@git-ssh.cdot.io/cdot/prisma-airs-desktop-v2.git`.\nCheckout: `/Users/cdot/development/redtail/prisma-airs-desktop-v2`.\n\n## Architectural guiding principles\n\n1. Design deep modules: substantial behavior behind a small domain interface.\n   Apply the deletion test; do not wrap every SDK method in a pass-through module.\n2. The interface is the test surface. Exercise production transitions and\n   adapters; use independent wire/protocol expectations for external contracts.\n3. One adapter is a hypothetical seam; two real adapters justify a seam. Shared\n   Academy presentation has real web/desktop consumers. Do not build speculative\n   browser/backend/plugin architectures for this desktop delivery.\n4. Rust owns authority and locality for tenant/credential/workspace context,\n   request lifecycle, configuration review, protected storage, and approvals.\n   React owns presentation and editable input, not permission to execute.\n5. The SDK owns its declared transport/authentication contracts. The application\n   owns interactive identity, OS storage, context admission, and workflow policy.\n   Do not duplicate SDK retry/pagination/OAuth policy without a documented need.\n6. Unknown outcomes and partial/stale/denied/unsupported data remain distinct.\n   Never map an unknown verdict to allow or a cancelled write to successful rollback.\n7. Host credentials and capabilities are explicit. No ambient environment-file\n   discovery, inherited production fallback, generic renderer secret access,\n   unrestricted native plugin grants, or cross-origin credential forwarding.\n8. Shared appearance does not imply shared learner state. Tenant operations,\n   Academy identity/progression, Harness inference identity, and MCP grants stay\n   separately scoped. A tenant switch does not sign the learner out of Academy.\n9. Keep provenance and reproducibility local to dependency selection. Exact\n   versions, source hashes, build inputs, notices, and protocol pins are evidence;\n   a neighboring active worktree is not a release dependency.\n10. Measure native usability/performance and the actual installed package. A\n    successful browser render or mocked IPC assertion is insufficient evidence\n    for Tauri host behavior, VoiceOver, Keychain, sidecar isolation, or notarization.\n\nConcrete interfaces, crate decomposition, shared package extraction, and storage\nformats are outputs of G01. This plan defines behavior and testable work, not\nunreviewed Rust signatures or a predetermined hierarchy of tiny modules.\n\n## Ordered phases and chunks\n\nEvery Cxx has its own instruction file under [chunks](chunks/README.md). Complete\nchunks in numeric order. A dependency delay blocks its chunk; report it honestly\nand continue only research/preparation explicitly within that chunk. Do not\nsilently advance a later phase or replace a real dependency with a fake to pass.\nFor a prolonged upstream stall, report the exact dependency and offer an owner\nreorder/scope decision. Do not wait indefinitely or reorder silently; an approved\nchange updates predecessors, plan and review evidence before execution.\n\n| Phase | Chunks   | Result                                                           |\n|-------|----------|------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| P00   | G00, G01 | Confirmed plan; selected and reviewed architecture               |\n| P01   | C01–C03  | Shared desktop shell, native core, first complete Runtime scan   |\n| P02   | C04–C07  | Academy identity, learner presentation, assessment, offline work |\n| P03   | C08–C12  | Runtime/DLP administration, Sessions, reports, Library           |\n| P04   | C13–C15  | Existing Red Team/Model reads and complete API Client workflows  |\n| P05   | C16–C18  | Gateway workspace/configuration, reviewed changes, inference     |\n| P06   | C19–C22  | Native Harness, Rust sign-in, typed host tools, workspace MCP    |\n| P07   | C23–C24  | Reviewed imports, native quality, accessibility/privacy closure  |\n| P08   | C25–C26  | Controlled live qualification; signed installed DMG acceptance   |\n| P09   | C27–C44  | UI v2 \"Refined Native\": spec, shell, wizard, destinations (D058); re-cut from C35 as one screen per chunk ported from the prototype (D059) |\n\n### G00 — Plan confirmation\n\nFinish the decision ledger, baseline coverage, chunk instructions, review/test\nprotocols, dependency gates, and architecture handoff. Obtain an actual independent\nplan review meeting D019. Product decisions are accepted and the owner explicitly\nselected the architecture candidates and source terms in D024/D025. Continue the\nrequested design work; no further blanket scope question is needed. Architecture\nreview and implementation evidence remain distinct gates.\n\n### G01 — Architecture improvement workflow\n\nFollow [the handoff](architecture-handoff.md) and the requested\nimprove-codebase-architecture skill. Explore the named areas, show a temporary\nHTML report with before/after candidates, ask the owner to choose, and grill the\nselected design. Produce ADRs, interface/test ownership, dependency/import\ndecisions, and concrete chunk command specifications. Review the architecture\nat least 9/10 in every critical dimension and overall. Revise this plan if the\nselected design affects it, then re-review affected planning evidence.\n\nNo application scaffolding, shared-source refactoring, real-data migration,\nproduct endpoint calls, or packaging implementation begins before G01 passes.\n\n## Per-chunk execution contract\n\n1. Read AGENTS/CLAUDE, glossary, accepted ADRs, the chunk, predecessor receipts,\n   dependency manifest, test catalogue, and its canonical implementer prompt.\n2. Confirm a clean approved source snapshot and identify unrelated user work.\n   Resolve dependency availability/provenance and baseline check limitations.\n3. Implement only that chunk and approved supporting changes. A shared Academy,\n   SDK, or Harness change needs its own reviewed revision and regression evidence;\n   preserve active work and do not move another project's main branch casually.\n4. Run exact checks from the approved command manifest. Use deterministic local\n   fixtures and literal external contracts, then the appropriate native tests.\n   Never call hosted application endpoints by accident.\n5. Record commands, environments, inputs, results, failures, hashes, limitations,\n   and rollback rehearsal. Save raw sensitive evidence privately; commit redacted\n   summaries with references/hashes, not credentials or customer bodies.\n6. Freeze source and dependency inputs. Request actual independent Codex\n   review through [the independent prompt](prompts/review-chunk.md) (D026).\n7. If any critical dimension/overall score is below 9, required fixes remain,\n   source changed, or mandatory evidence is absent: remediate, rerun affected\n   checks, freeze a new snapshot, and request a new review of the same chunk.\n8. Complete only after the receipt and evidence validate against current inputs.\n   After a phase's last chunk, obtain the cumulative Codex phase review\n   ([prompt](prompts/review-phase.md)) before starting the next phase.\n   Make a focused commit; revalidate that its tree matches the reviewed tree.\n   Later edits invalidate affected receipts and trigger targeted regression\n   checks/re-review. Preserve failed attempts; never pressure a reviewer to pass.\n\n## Command contract to establish in C01\n\nThese commands are a required future interface, not tools that exist today:\n\n```text\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- check --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- test --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- native --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- review --chunk Cxx --reviewer codex --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- review --phase Pxx --reviewer codex --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- complete --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- package --kind preview --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- package --kind release-candidate --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\n```\n\nC01 creates the controller and a versioned manifest of exact commands, working\ndirectories, targets, features, fixtures, timeouts, and expected evidence. It\ncannot implement the later chunks itself. G01 specifies actual Rust/pnpm/Tauri\ncommands after choosing the workspace; a future engineer must not guess them or\nclaim these placeholder commands already ran. Document/test the controller's\nrejection of missing, stale, malformed, edited, or sub-threshold receipts.\n\n## Coverage and evidence\n\n- [Feature baseline](feature-baseline.md): existing working/preview/stub behavior.\n- [Parity matrix](parity-matrix.md): work ownership and release closure.\n- [Dependency gates](dependencies.md): accepted inputs and unresolved qualification.\n- [Test catalogue](test-catalogue.md): named behavioral/native/artifact test cases.\n- [Review protocol](review-protocol.md): scoring, receipt fields, invalidation.\n- [Release contract](release-contract.md): live scope, signing, package/install proof.\n- [Mandatory live checks](live-checks.md): fixed L-family/F-smoke requirements and\n  owner-approved per-workflow live scope; no access-driven silent reductions.\n- [Distribution gate](distribution.md): owner-selected source/binary terms,\n  Academy permission, framework disposition and exact shipped notices.\n- [Status](status.md): authoritative current state; planned is not implemented.\n\nG01 must convert each chunk's named tests into exact invocation commands and\nobservable assertions. Tests cannot simply repeat implementation decisions.\nFresh signed-artifact checks bind the final DMG hash and installed bytes; source\nor fixture checks do not substitute for them.\n\n## Expected sequencing dependencies\n\nThe scan proof can use accepted SDK Phase 3 after native macOS qualification.\nManagement/DLP uses accepted Phase 4 or a later explicitly reviewed compatible\npin. Existing Red Team/Model reads need verified Phase 5 capabilities; complete\nGateway needs Phase 6 capabilities. Harness/Academy transports are application\nwork, not delivered by the SCM SDK. Keep these upstream dependencies visible and\ndo not require unrelated SDK operations merely because they share a library.\n\nFirst-slice metrics establish named budgets before C04. Every UI chunk includes\nkeyboard/focus checks. Later introducing chunks register their numeric workload\nbudgets before implementation/acceptance measurement under T20 governance; budget\nchanges need recorded rationale and owner or independent reviewer acknowledgement.\nC24/C26 compare these pre-registered values, not newly fitted limits. C24 closes\nthe full native/VoiceOver/performance and cross-feature offline matrix.\nPreview packages may demonstrate accepted scope but must name missing features.\nOnly C26 can establish stable installed-package readiness.\n\n## Completion and delivery\n\nStable v2 requires every agreed working-v1 parity row closed, all chunk/phase\nreviews passing, designated live acceptance complete, no missing mandatory\nchecks, and C26's installed signed/notarized DMG evidence. V1 stays available;\nfresh credentials and reviewed imports remain explicit. Updates initially use\nmanual signed DMGs with rollback instructions.\n\nThe active implementation task may deliver reviewed Git commits to the exact\nowner-specified remote using normal pushes. Application release publication,\nhosting changes, and promotion over v1 must remain within the owner's explicit\nrelease instruction; preparing a signed package does not by itself publish it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
